 "answer": {
  "answer": "The goal was not met. Repeats confirmed two of the five settings the brief asked for, and only one of those is clearly the policy's failure. On 096988dd (S2), with the car 2 s earlier at 2x speed and the ego at 1.4x, the policy steered left out of its lane and hit the car beside it in every kept run. That range only just clears 0.5. On 1c45ce3a (S30), with the car 1.5 s later at 1.0x and the ego at 0.8x, every run also failed. In most of those runs, though, a replayed car (actor 24) merged into the ego from the right after the slower ego had fallen about 20 m behind its recorded position. That points to a side effect of replaying traffic that doesn't react to the ego, more than a policy fault. The best unconfirmed candidate is 187e6244 (S20: car 2 s later at 1.0x, ego 1.2x). All of its failures were the policy drifting left into a car coming up alongside, but its range still runs below 0.5. Seven of the ten scenes had no failures at any setting tried, and most settings ran only once, so their ranges stay wide.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "On 096988dd, with the car 2 s earlier at 2x speed and the ego at 1.4x, every kept run failed, and each failure was the policy's fault. The ego steered left against a STRAIGHT/RIGHT route command and hit actor 41 in the left lane. This setting is confirmed, though its lower bound is only just above 0.5.",
    "settings": [
     "S2"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "On 1c45ce3a, with the car 1.5 s later at 1.0x speed and the ego at 0.8x, every run failed and the setting counts as confirmed. However, only one failure was the policy's fault (the ego drifted right into actor 24). In the others, actor 24 merged left into an ego that was about 20 m behind its recorded position. Those were judged not the policy's fault or unclear, so this case mostly reflects non-reactive replay, not a policy weakness.",
    "settings": [
     "S30"
    ]
   },
   {
    "claim": "The other failures on 1c45ce3a were single runs, all at ego 0.8x. Each had the same cause: actor 24 hit the ego from the right, with fault judged unclear or no. On that scene, single runs at ego 0.6x and 1.2x did not fail. These failures add nothing new: they share S30's scene, so they can't count as distinct cases, and they share its replay explanation.",
